"All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","All University Colleges","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","DI-UC","DI-UC-ALL","Hospitals","KP","Other Hospitals","Universities","University Colleges","University of Copenhagen","copenhagen university hospital - holbæk"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-e1f30dbf-094a-4633-8cf5-8387c60593ef"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Effects of caloric restriction with different doses of exercise on fat loss in people living with type 2 diabetes : A secondary analysis of the DOSE-EX randomized clinical trial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-811279","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"In recent years, business-to-business firms have experienced increasing uncertainty, disruptive events, and major crises that have challenged their businesses. While these developments have triggered a focus on firms' preparedness to handle uncertainty, surprisingly little has been said about preparedness in an interorganizational context. This oversight is noteworthy, as interorganizational contexts are not only the dominant settings within business markets but also key drivers of the development of resilience and responsiveness. This conceptual paper outlines the concept of preparedness in business-to-business relationships and suggests a research agenda for interorganizational preparedness—an important concept in a fast-changing and uncertain business environment.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Kleinaltenkamp, Michael","Pedersen, Carsten Lund","Ritter, Thomas"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-827212"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Ritter, Thomas"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Freie Universität Berlin"],"keyword":["Disruption","Interorganizational","Preparedness","Relationship","Resilience","Responsiveness","Uncertainty"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"interorganizational preparedness in business to business relationships","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.indmarman.2025.07.006","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-827212","identifier":[{"id":"105011662843","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["cbs-f5747456-8c0d-42af-8980-940b1ed7098d"],"keyword":["Disruption","Interorganizational","Preparedness","Relationship","Resilience","Responsiveness","Uncertainty"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-1FC6F20861FE70EB6356EF952DD08D2E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/117496230/1-s2.0-S0019850125001051-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.indmarman.2025.07.006","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"freie universität berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Freie Universität Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Strategy and Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"kleinaltenkamppau","last":"Kleinaltenkamp","name":"Kleinaltenkamp, Michael","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Carsten Lund","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","name":"Pedersen, Carsten Lund","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"ritterpau","last":"Ritter","locid":"69cce423-3a9c-428c-9f55-37e31f887596","name":"Ritter, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0003-4526-1573","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.indmarman.2025.07.006","issn":["00198501","18732062"],"issn_org":["00198501"],"journal":"Industrial Marketing Management","pages":"97-103","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Industrial Marketing Management","volume":"129"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105011662843"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-f5747456-8c0d-42af-8980-940b1ed7098d"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Interorganizational Preparedness in Business-to-business Relationships","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-827212","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"This chapter examines the intricate relationship between theorizing and practice within the context of business-to-business marketing. We introduce the concept of engaged scholarship, a collaborative approach where academics and practitioners jointly address complex real-world challenges. The core premise of engaged scholarship is the establishment of common ground, allowing for effective knowledge co-creation by actors from both domains. We posit that theories underpin practical decision-making processes, and illustrate how both academic theorists and industry practitioners inherently operate with theories, whether implicitly or explicitly. We highlight the contrasting criteria for theory assessment between practitioners, who prioritize practical contributions, and academics, who emphasize theoretical rigor and advancement. To overcome the inherent trade-offs between practitioners and academics we need an ambidextrous approach, denoting the capability to excel in both theorizing and practical application. We advocate for the cultivation of engagement ambidexterity, presenting four organizational approaches: structural separation; task partitioning; temporal separation; and contextual enabling. This chapter underscores the interconnected nature of theorizing and practice in business-to-business marketing, emphasizing the necessity of ambidextrous individuals and organizations to bridge academia and industry effectively. These strategies offer avenues to foster engagement ambidexterity, facilitating a harmonious balance between theoretical rigor and practical relevance.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Ritter, Thomas","Pedersen, Carsten Lund"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-774700"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Academic practitioner gap","Ambidexterity","B2B marketing","Engaged scholarship"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"building engagement ambidexterity a call for proftitioners and pracfessors","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-774700","identifier":[{"id":"85216187183","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["cbs-dea04c24-d3a9-40b5-b1c6-d8cb30a2a319"],"keyword":["Academic-practitioner gap","Ambidexterity","B2B marketing","Engaged scholarship"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-4-CF3412C8A63E22F548ED9980E6FF3E29"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Strategy and Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"ritterpau","last":"Ritter","locid":"69cce423-3a9c-428c-9f55-37e31f887596","name":"Ritter, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0003-4526-1573","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Carsten Lund","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","name":"Pedersen, Carsten Lund","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Michel","key":"vanderborghpedh","last":"van der Borgh","name":"van der Borgh, Michel","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Adam","key":"lindgreenpedh","last":"Lindgreen","name":"Lindgreen, Adam","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Tobias","key":"schäferspedh","last":"Schäfers","name":"Schäfers, Tobias","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4337/9781800888531.00014","eisbn":["9781800888531"],"isbn":["9781800888524"],"pages":"95-109","place":"Cheltenham","publisher":"Edward Elgar Publishing","series":"How To Guides","status":"p","subtitle":"A Guide to Purposeful Marketing Research","title":"How to Achieve Societal Impact Through Engaged and Collaborative Scholarship"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85216187183"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-dea04c24-d3a9-40b5-b1c6-d8cb30a2a319"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Building Engagement Ambidexterity : A Call for Proftitioners and Pracfessors","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-774700","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Despite segmentation's importance for marketing in general and the extensive research published on the topic over several decades, business-to-business segmentation is still not fully developed as a general theory—a status that may decrease academic interest in the field. Based on a recent contribution on the definition and typology of theory, this paper explores the degree to which segmentation can be considered a theory and, more specifically, which elements and perspectives are currently underdeveloped. We highlight some fundamental challenges that must be addressed to establish a better theoretical foundation for segmentation's evolution into a more developed theory. We also present questions for future research to guide the advancement of business-to-business segmentation, which is necessary for a concept so central to business-to-business marketing.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Ritter, Thomas","Pedersen, Carsten Lund"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-704415"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Ritter, Thomas"],"keyword":["Buying behavior","Customer need","Research","Segmentation","Theory","Theory Customer need"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci","soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"is segmentation a theory improving the theoretical basis of a foundational concept in business to business marketing","src":["cbs","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.indmarman.2023.11.012","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-704415","identifier":[{"id":"85179171519","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["cbs-7a359373-5777-4491-b3cd-6c452a1f44c0","itu-1f2e4b4d-12df-421a-9463-1ca3b64937e4"],"keyword":["Buying behavior","Customer need","Research","Segmentation","Theory","Theory Customer need"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology","Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-0487DB8F521DE94FD34406E8AA13F1AA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102946121/1-s2.0-S0019850123002286-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/99414786/ritter_thomas_et_al_is_segmentation_a_theory_publisherversion.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.indmarman.2023.11.012","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"rem","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0019850123002286","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Strategy and Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"ritterpau","last":"Ritter","locid":"69cce423-3a9c-428c-9f55-37e31f887596","name":"Ritter, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0003-4526-1573","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Carsten Lund","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","locid":"5f3b7745-99be-4d1b-8ce1-a413325c2008","name":"Lund Pedersen, Carsten","name_org":"Pedersen, Carsten Lund","orcid":"0000-0002-9328-9784","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.indmarman.2023.11.012","issn":["00198501","18732062"],"issn_org":["00198501"],"journal":"Industrial Marketing Management","pages":"82-92","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Industrial Marketing Management","volume":"116"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85179171519"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-7a359373-5777-4491-b3cd-6c452a1f44c0","itu-1f2e4b4d-12df-421a-9463-1ca3b64937e4"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Is Segmentation a Theory? : Improving the Theoretical Basis of a Foundational Concept in Business-to-business Marketing","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-704415","year":"2024","yearsub":2024}
{"abstract":"This paper adopts a project-based perspective to analyze how strategic renewal evolves in organizations over time. Projects are temporary resource-committing activities that deliberately impose changes on existing business operations and, thereby, generate strategic renewal. Projects can be empirically identified and are, therefore, suited for analyzing manifestations of deliberate and emergent strategies driven by employee behaviors that either comply with or deviate from the official strategy. In the paper, we present ten theoretically derived project categories that can be used to analyze strategic renewal through the enactment of different projects. We also discuss the implications and limitations of the project-based perspective for strategy research and management practice.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Lund Pedersen, Carsten","Ritter, Thomas","Andersen, Torben Juul"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-287082"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Compliance","Deviance","Emergence","Initiative","Project","Strategic renewal","Strategy formulation"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a project based perspective on strategic renewal","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1561/111.00000069","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-287082","identifier":[{"id":"105000753750","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["cbs-7a873117-533f-4c72-8c84-57959ca8e00a"],"keyword":["Compliance","Deviance","Emergence","Initiative","Project","Strategic renewal","Strategy formulation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-A8C5CD4D6C1BE589621BFF4E163C7CB5"],"oa_link"